<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Voltaire</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1694">1694</a>–<a href="/years/1778">1778</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as François-Marie Arouet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Voltaire, the pen name of François-Marie Arouet, was a French writer and philosopher and the most famous figure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>. In plays, histories, satires such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candide">Candide</a> and polemics he attacked religious intolerance, the power of the Church and arbitrary government. He corresponded with rulers including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and spent his last decades near the Swiss border at Ferney.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/voltaire.jpg" alt="Voltaire" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicolas de Largillière · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolas_de_Largilli%C3%A8re_-_Portrait_de_Voltaire_(1694-1778)_en_1718_-_P208_-_mus%C3%A9e_Carnavalet_-_5_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>François-Marie Arouet, later known as Voltaire, was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, the youngest of five children of François Arouet, a lawyer and minor treasury official, and Marie Marguerite Daumard, whose family was of the lowest rank of the French nobility. He gave his birth date as 20 February 1694, claiming to be the illegitimate son of a nobleman, but his birth is dated to 21 November 1694, with a baptism on 22 November. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> educated him at the Collège Louis-le-Grand from 1704 to 1711, where he was taught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, theology and rhetoric.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Voltaire's play Artémire flopped in 1720, and he turned to an epic poem about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry IV of France</a>, La Henriade. Denied a licence to publish it, he went north in August 1722 to find a publisher outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> he was impressed by the openness and tolerance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> society. The poem was printed clandestinely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a>, and when copies were smuggled into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> it was an instant success. His play Mariamne failed in 1724 but did better in a reworked version in April 1725.</p><p>In early 1726 Guy Auguste de Rohan-Chabot had Voltaire beaten by servants after a quarrel about his name change. When Voltaire challenged him to a duel, the powerful Rohan family had him imprisoned without trial in the Bastille on 17 April 1726. Fearing indefinite imprisonment, he asked to be exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> instead, and on 2 May he was escorted from the Bastille to Calais and embarked.</p><p>He was one of the first authors to be commercially successful internationally, and wrote more than 20,000 letters and 2,000 books and pamphlets. In February 1778 he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> for the first time in over 25 years, partly for the opening of his tragedy Irène, and died there on 30 May 1778. The Church had refused him a Christian burial because he would not retract his criticism, so friends buried him secretly at the Abbey of Scellières.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Voltaire produced plays, poems, novels, essays, histories and scientific expositions. His best-known work is the novella <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candide">Candide</a> (1759), which ridicules Gottfried Leibniz's belief that this is the best of all possible worlds. His History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII</a> (1731), The Age of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> (1751) and Essay on the Customs and the Spirit of the Nations (1756) set aside diplomatic and military narrative for customs, social history and the arts and sciences, and treated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> as a whole.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Voltaire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Enlightenment of Montesquieu, <b>Voltaire</b>, Rousseau and Diderot's Encyclopédie was largely conducted in French.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Frederick II, who corresponded with <b>Voltaire</b> and wrote on politics, history and war, became the model of the enlightened absolutist.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>John Gabriel Stedman's illustrated Narrative of a Five Years Expedition against the Revolted Negroes of Surinam, published in London in 1796 with engravings by William Blake among others, shaped European debates on slavery, and <b>Voltaire</b> set the scene of a mutilated slave in Candide of 1759 near Suriname.</blockquote><a href="/empire/suriname">Suriname · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Confucian ethics, centred on humaneness, reciprocity and the duties of relationships, offered an alternative to ethics founded on divine command, and the Jesuit missionaries who translated the Analects into Latin in the 17th century made Confucius admired by thinkers of the European Enlightenment such as Leibniz and <b>Voltaire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/confucianism">Confucianism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Locke’s Letter Concerning Toleration of 1689 argued that the state had no business forcing belief, and <b>Voltaire</b>’s Treatise on Tolerance of 1763 was written after the Protestant merchant Jean Calas was executed in Toulouse in 1762 on a false charge of murdering his son to stop him becoming…</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Voltaire</b> popularised it in France, Émilie du Châtelet translated the Principia into French, and the confidence that reason and experiment could uncover the laws of nature, and perhaps of society, passed into the 18th century.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Outcomes</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1748" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Voltaire"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1748">What was here in 1748? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;There the Jew, the Mahometan, and the Christian transact together, as though they all professed the same religion, and give the name of infidel to none but bankrupts.&#8221;</p><footer>Letters on the English, letter 6 &#183; English translation of 1733 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2445/pg2445-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If one religion only were allowed in England, the Government would very possibly become arbitrary; if there were but two, the people would cut one another’s throats; but as there are such a multitude, they all live happy and in peace.&#8221;</p><footer>Letters on the English, letter 6 &#183; English translation of 1733 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2445/pg2445-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I and my children cultivate them; our labour preserves us from three great evils—weariness, vice, and want.&#8221;</p><footer>Candide, chapter 30 &#183; anonymous translation (Boni and Liveright, 1918) &#183; spoken by an old Turk in the tale &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/19942/pg19942-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;There is no doubt of it; but in this country it is found good, from time to time, to kill one Admiral to encourage the others.&#8221;</p><footer>Candide, chapter 23 &#183; anonymous translation (Boni and Liveright, 1918) &#183; an Englishman at Portsmouth, on the shooting of Admiral Byng &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/19942/pg19942-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If this is the best of possible worlds, what then are the others?&#8221;</p><footer>Candide, chapter 6 &#183; anonymous translation (Boni and Liveright, 1918) &#183; spoken by Candide &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/19942/pg19942-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;He proved admirably that there is no effect without a cause, and that, in this best of all possible worlds, the Baron's castle was the most magnificent of castles, and his lady the best of all possible Baronesses.&#8221;</p><footer>Candide, chapter 1 &#183; anonymous translation (Boni and Liveright, 1918) &#183; of the philosopher Pangloss &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/19942/pg19942-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
